    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Morton was founded in 1923 when land agent Morton Smith staked the townsite and named it after himself.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Morton%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In May 1924, Morton won the county seat election, defeating Ligon by a 79‑to‑20 vote margin.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/morton-tx-cochran-county"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town was platted in 1923, and Smith’s land office sat on the east side of the town square.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Morton%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Unlike many Texas towns that vanished after railroad routes bypassed them, Morton survived the 1930s and 1940s railroad decline.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Morton%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Morton’s motto, “The Last Frontier,” reflects its late settlement and the conversion of ranch land into farms during the 1920s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Morton%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Morton is the county seat of Cochran County, a status that helped attract new farming families and keep the town thriving.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20,
        29
      ],
